WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:06.320
The next keyword we will look at allows us to&nbsp;
return records between two numbers or two dates.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:10.160
The actual keyword is the BETWEEN&nbsp;
keyword. For this example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:18.080
I want this query to return orders from June 2014.&nbsp;
To accomplish this, I will add a WHERE clause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:29.840
to the end of this query. Then, I will add the&nbsp;
following: OrderDate BETWEEN '06/01/2014' and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:39.440
'06/30/2014'. I will add single quotes around&nbsp;
the dates since they have slashes in them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:47.520
Otherwise, SQL will think you are trying to do a&nbsp;
division problem. I will run this, and we now see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:54.240
orders between those two dates. There is one&nbsp;
real-life situation I want to point out here:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:03.280
This date format assumes 12 AM. If the date field&nbsp;
you are using keeps track of time, you will want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:10.240
to reflect that in any date criteria. For example,&nbsp;
if we saw that these dates had timestamps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:16.800
we would want to change the end date to reflect&nbsp;
11:59:00 PM, as I am doing with this criterion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:25.840
Otherwise, you will not see any orders on&nbsp;
June 30 unless they were placed at midnight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:31.040
That said, the BETWEEN keyword is inclusive&nbsp;
with a number range or date range,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:37.040
as we do have orders from&nbsp;
June 30 in this result set.

